#+TITLE: Native Windows Emacs release build needs writable temp and explicit UTF-8
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-28

* Reproduction

On pfenny, Windows MSYS2 over SSH, source =scripts/environnement-windows.sh=
then run =make release install collect-artefacts= at 2.3.75 (upstream
=1ad230be=). The resolved Emacs is the native mingw64 executable.
The SSH environment does not define TMPDIR, TEMP or TMP.

1. =org-texinfo-supports-math-p= calls =make-temp-file= during the dialect
   warnings Texinfo export. Emacs selects the drive root and fails with
   =permission-denied (Creating file with prefix Permission denied c:/test)=.
2. After supplying a writable user temp directory, =build-paged-spec.el=
   prompts =Select coding system (default utf-8)= when writing
   =build/pages/1-brief-history.txt=. Its inherited coding system is
   =iso-latin-1-dos=. In batch the prompt fails with =Error reading from stdin=.

The source pages contain non-Latin-1 characters. This is a build environment
failure, unrelated to the AutoLISP reader or SCHMS DCL.

* Evidence and workaround

=scripts/run-emacs.sh= normalizes Windows temp paths to POSIX paths without
checking whether the selected Emacs is a POSIX or native Windows executable.
The disposable build wrapper supplies TMPDIR/TEMP/TMP as Windows paths to an
existing writable user temp directory and sets the Emacs language environment
to UTF-8. The text and Org page generators then each emit 4652 pages.

Adding =--eval= ahead of =--script= is not safe: Emacs reorders script loading
and the custom script sees the eval option in =command-line-args-left=.
The temporary wrapper maps =--script= to =--load= before adding its eval.
A durable fix should keep the documented script invocation intact.

* Acceptance

- Run release documentation under native and POSIX Emacs on Windows.
- Cover missing temp variables and Windows-form temp variables; require a
  writable temp directory, without writing to the drive root.
- Write all generated text/Org/HTML/reference files explicitly as UTF-8.
- No interactive encoding prompts in batch, including non-Latin-1 input.
- Preserve script arguments and check the full release/install/collection.
